SELECT 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_ISHIYAMA_TEXT', 						'잇코잇키는 15~16세기 일본에서 봉건 영주와 사무라이 통치에 대항하여 봉기한 불교 승려, 농민, 지역 무사들의 무장 집단이었습니다. 정토진종(진정한 정토) 종파의 가르침을 중심으로 그들은 종교적 헌신과 사회적 평등에 대한 열망으로 뭉쳤습니다. 혼란스러운 전국 시대에 등장한 이 연맹은 여러 지방에 자치 공동체를 설립했으며, 특히 오사카 근처의 이시야마 혼간지 요새 사원을 통제했습니다. 그들의 강력한 저항은 16세기 후반에 결국 운동을 진압한 오다 노부나가를 포함한 강력한 군벌들에게 도전했습니다. 비록 패배했지만 잇코잇키는 신앙과 정치적 저항을 결합하여 일본 역사상 가장 초기의 대규모 풀뿌리 운동 중 하나로 지속적인 유산을 남겼습니다.';

/*
Atolan
The Amis, also known as Pangcah, are the largest Indigenous group in Taiwan, traditionally inhabiting the island''s eastern coastal plains and river valleys from Hualien to Taitung. With a history stretching back thousands of years, the Amis are part of the Austronesian cultural and linguistic family, sharing deep ancestral ties with other Pacific Island peoples. Traditionally, they lived in matrilineal societies organized around extended families and age-grade systems, with men''s and women''s roles clearly defined through communal rituals and agricultural practices such as rice and millet farming. Their rich oral traditions, music, and dance, especially the ilisin harvest festival, reflect their close relationship with nature and the spiritual world. During the Japanese colonial period (1895–1945) and later under Chinese rule, the Amis faced assimilation pressures, yet they have preserved much of their language and customs. Today, the Amis are at the forefront of Taiwan''s Indigenous cultural revival, advocating for land rights, education, and the recognition of their heritage within the broader national identity.

Cavanbah
The Bundjalung (also spelled Bungjalung) are an Aboriginal Australian people whose traditional lands span the northern coast and hinterlands of New South Wales and southeastern Queensland, encompassing areas around Byron Bay, Lismore, and the Clarence and Richmond Rivers. With a history extending tens of thousands of years, the Bundjalung developed a rich cultural life centered on their deep connection to Country (ngalingah jugun), expressed through language, songlines, and ceremonial practices tied to the land and waterways. Traditionally, they lived as skilled hunters, fishers, and gatherers, with complex kinship systems and spiritual beliefs grounded in the Dreaming. Following European colonization in the 19th century, the Bundjalung experienced dispossession, violence, and disruption of cultural traditions, yet they have continued to maintain and revive their language and heritage. Today, Bundjalung people play a vital role in cultural preservation, land management, and advocacy for Indigenous rights, ensuring the survival and renewal of one of Australia''s oldest living cultures.

Mangola
The Hadza, also known as Hadzabe, are an Indigenous hunter-gatherer people living around Lake Eyasi in northern Tanzania, and are considered one of the last remaining groups in the world to maintain a traditional foraging lifestyle. Their ancestry in the East African Rift Valley likely stretches back tens of thousands of years, linking them to some of the earliest human populations. The Hadza live in small, mobile bands and subsist on wild foods (such as tubers, berries, honey, and game)n without agriculture, herding, or permanent dwellings. Their egalitarian social structure emphasizes sharing, cooperation, and autonomy, with decisions made collectively and little hierarchy. Despite pressures from land encroachment, conservation zones, and modernization, the Hadza have resisted full assimilation and continue to embody a way of life closely connected to the rhythms of nature. Today, they are recognized both for their deep anthropological significance in understanding human origins and for their ongoing struggle to protect their ancestral lands and cultural autonomy.

Melgitat
The Beothuk were an Indigenous people of Newfoundland, Canada, who lived on the island for thousands of years before European contact, relying on fishing, hunting, and gathering to sustain themselves in the island''s coastal and forested environments. Known for their distinctive use of red ochre to paint their bodies, tools, and dwellings (a practice that gave rise to their name, meaning "the people") the Beothuk maintained a largely self-sufficient and isolated existence, avoiding direct contact with European settlers and fishermen who arrived from the 16th century onward. As colonial expansion, resource competition, and introduced diseases spread, the Beothuk were gradually displaced from their lands and deprived of access to vital food sources. By the early 19th century, their population had dwindled severely, and with the death of Shanawdithit in 1829, the Beothuk as a distinct people were declared extinct. However, their memory endures as a poignant symbol of cultural loss and the lasting impact of colonization in North America.
*/
